{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"objects","title":"Objects","group":"Arrays & Objects","order":24,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Objects are collections of key → value properties. Keys are strings or symbols; values can be anything, including functions (methods).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Object literals, modern syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const name = \"Asha\", age = 28;\nconst field = \"email\";\nconst user = {\n  name,                                  // shorthand for name: name\n  age,\n  [field]: \"asha@example.com\",           // computed key\n  [`is${\"Admin\"}`]: false,\n  \"full name\": \"Asha Rao\",               // any string can be a key\n  greet() { return `Hi, ${this.name}`; },  // method shorthand\n};\nconsole.log(user);\nconsole.log(user.greet(), user[\"full name\"], user[field]);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{\n  name: 'Asha',\n  age: 28,\n  email: 'asha@example.com',\n  isAdmin: false,\n  'full name': 'Asha Rao',\n  greet: [Function: greet]\n}\nHi, Asha Asha Rao asha@example.com</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-edit\"></i>Reading, writing, deleting, checking</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const car = { brand: \"Tata\", year: 2020 };\ncar.color = \"red\";           // add\ncar.year = 2021;             // update\ndelete car.brand;            // remove\nconsole.log(car);\nconsole.log(\"color\" in car, Object.hasOwn(car, \"color\"), car.speed);\nconsole.log(\"toString\" in car, Object.hasOwn(car, \"toString\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{ year: 2021, color: 'red' }\ntrue true undefined\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Own props</th><th>Inherited props</th><th>Value <code>undefined</code> counts?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>obj.key !== undefined</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No — misses explicit <code>undefined</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'key' in obj</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>Object.hasOwn(obj, 'key')</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>Keys are strings (or symbols)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const obj = {};\nobj[1] = \"number key\";\nobj[{}] = \"object key\";\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj), obj[\"1\"]);\nconst order = { b: 1, 2: \"two\", a: 2, 1: \"one\" };\nconsole.log(Object.keys(order));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ '1', '[object Object]' ] number key\n[ '1', '2', 'b', 'a' ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Property order: integer-like keys first in ascending order, then string keys in insertion order, then symbols. Need real non-string keys? Use a <code>Map</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>Getters and setters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const temperature = {\n  celsius: 25,\n  get fahrenheit() {\n    return this.celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;\n  },\n  set fahrenheit(f) {\n    this.celsius = Math.round((f - 32) * 5 / 9);\n  },\n};\nconsole.log(temperature.fahrenheit);\ntemperature.fahrenheit = 212;\nconsole.log(temperature.celsius, temperature);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>77\n100 { celsius: 100, fahrenheit: [Getter/Setter] }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Iterating an object</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const scores = { math: 90, art: 75, music: 88 };\nfor (const [subject, score] of Object.entries(scores)) {\n  console.log(`${subject.padEnd(6)} ${score}`);\n}\nconst total = Object.values(scores).reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0);\nconsole.log(total, Object.keys(scores).length);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>math   90\nart    75\nmusic  88\n253 3</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you check if an object is empty?</summary><div><p><code>Object.keys(obj).length === 0</code> (own enumerable string keys). For a stricter check include symbols and non-enumerables: <code>Reflect.ownKeys(obj).length === 0</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Difference between dot and bracket notation?</summary><div><p>Dot needs a valid identifier known when writing the code (<code>obj.name</code>). Brackets take any expression evaluated at runtime (<code>obj[key]</code>, <code>obj['full name']</code>, <code>obj[sym]</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}